# Architecture principles (user standard)

All architecture artifacts, plans, and handoffs follow **Clean Architecture** (Uncle Bob) and use **design patterns where they reduce coupling or clarify boundaries**—never pattern soup.

## Dependency rule (non-negotiable)

Source dependencies point **inward only**. Inner rings never depend on UI, DB, HTTP, frameworks, or messaging.

| Ring | Contains | May depend on |
|------|----------|---------------|
| Entities | Enterprise rules, invariants | — |
| Use cases | Application orchestration; **ports** (interfaces) | Entities |
| Interface adapters | Controllers, presenters, repository/gateway **implementations** | Use cases via ports |
| Frameworks & drivers | Web stack, ORM, queues, filesystem | Adapters only |

**Checks on every design:** screaming architecture (structure reflects use cases, not framework); business logic not in controllers/ORM entities; frameworks swappable via ports; tests at entity/use-case level without I/O.

## SOLID and modularity

- **SRP** — one reason to change per module/class.
- **OCP** — extend via new adapters implementing existing ports, not edits to use cases.
- **LSP / ISP / DIP** — depend on abstractions (ports); keep interfaces small and role-specific.
- **Cohesion in, coupling out** — modules communicate only through declared ports or stable DTOs at boundaries.
- **No shared “utils” dump** — shared kernel only for true cross-cutting domain concepts.

## Design patterns (when appropriate)

Assign patterns to solve recurring boundary problems; document non-obvious choices as **ADRs** (decision, rationale, rejected alternatives).

| Need | Patterns |
|------|----------|
| Object creation without concrete coupling | Factory, Builder |
| Varying algorithm/policy | Strategy |
| External shape ≠ domain | Adapter, Anti-Corruption Layer |
| Request handling | Command, Chain of Responsibility |
| Complex subsystem at edge | Facade |
| Cross-cutting at **outer** ring only | Decorator, middleware |
| Read/write divergence (if justified) | CQRS |
| Multi-step distributed workflow (if justified) | Saga |

Avoid: anemic domain with all logic in services unless the problem is truly transaction-script style; Singleton except via DI scope.

## Extensibility and maintainability

**Humans:** predictable package layout, use-case-sized units, thin adapters, tests that document behavior (fast domain/use-case tests; contract tests on adapters).

**Machines (AI/tooling):** stable folder conventions, typed ports, explicit error/state models, `ARCHITECTURE.md` with “where new code goes,” machine-readable edge contracts (OpenAPI/AsyncAPI/events) where applicable.

## Documentation (required in designs and handoffs)

| Artifact | Purpose |
|----------|---------|
| Architecture section in plan | Context, goals, layer diagram, dependency rule, key ADRs |
| `ARCHITECTURE.md` (target repo) | Layer map, allowed dependencies, extension points |
| Module README per package | Responsibility, ports in/out, test approach |
| `docs/adr/NNN-title.md` | One decision per file |
| Port/interface docs | Preconditions, errors, idempotency, side effects |
| Use case briefs | Trigger, inputs, outputs, invariants |

Document **why** and **invariants**; code documents **how** for straightforward logic. Handoffs must list **documentation acceptance** (files to create/update) per package.

## Architectagent outputs must include

1. Layer/module map obeying the dependency rule  
2. Use case list with ports per use case  
3. Pattern/ADR notes only where earned  
4. Extension points (ports + adapter slots)  
5. Documentation checklist for implementers  
6. Handoffs that forbid business logic in outer rings and require layer-appropriate tests  

If user names a stack, align package naming in handoffs to that ecosystem while preserving the rings above.
